 A singly linked list is a queue when the items enter after its last cell and
 leave from its first one, since the removal of the first cell and the
 insertion after the last one need no predecessor (see Lemma 1 of
 \ref theory_sequence); the list keeps a pointer to each of the two
 cells, and an empty list has both nil. The deque needs instead the removal
 of the last cell too, i.e., its predecessor, which a bidirectional list has
 at hand. Among the vectors, a queue whose items stayed in the places from 0
 on would move all of them at each `dequeue()`, i.e., \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$; a
 circular vector lets the front move instead, as follows.

 \section theory_queue_circular Circular vectors

 In a circular vector \f$ A[ 0 .. m - 1 ] \f$ the place 0 follows the place
 \f$ m - 1 \f$, and a queue of \f$ n \le m \f$ items with its front at the
 place \f$ h \f$ holds the item of rank \f$ i \f$ from the front, for
 \f$ 0 \le i < n \f$, at the place
 \f[
   ( h + i ) \bmod m , \tag{1}
 \f]
 so that its back is at \f$ ( h + n - 1 ) \bmod m \f$ and the next item enters
 at \f$ ( h + n ) \bmod m \f$. A `dequeue()` advances \f$ h \f$ by one place
 and an `enqueue()` writes after the back, each in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$; the items
 run through the vector as a band that wraps around its end, and the
 vector is full when \f$ n = m \f$, which a vector with the two indices of the
 front and of the back alone could not tell from the empty one (both have
 the indices equal), whence the count \f$ n \f$ kept beside \f$ h \f$. Since
 \f$ h < m \f$ and \f$ n \le m \f$, the sum \f$ h + n \f$ is below
 \f$ 2 m \f$, and the modulo of (1) is a comparison and a subtraction rather
 than a division. A copy of `ArrayQueue` reads the items by (1) and writes
 them from the place 0 of its vector, \f$ h = 0 \f$, while a move takes the
 vector and leaves \f$ n = m = 0 \f$ behind, a queue that is empty and full
 at once, so that its contracts refuse both `dequeue()` and `enqueue()`.

 `RingDeque` takes \f$ m = 2^j \f$, so that the modulo is the mask
 \f$ m - 1 \f$, and keeps the front and the back as counters modulo
 \f$ 2^{64} \f$ whose difference is \f$ n \f$: an insertion at the back and a
 removal from the front change one counter each, and the place of a counter
 \f$ c \f$ is \f$ c \mathbin{\&} ( m - 1 ) \f$. When the vector is full it
 doubles, and its items are moved in order to the front of the new vector,
 in at most two contiguous pieces, \f$ A[ h .. m ) \f$ and the part that
 wrapped around; by the argument of the vectors that double (see
 \ref theory_sequence_amortized), \f$ n \f$ insertions move fewer than
 \f$ 2 n \f$ items. An insertion at the front of a full deque puts the new
 item at the last place of the new vector, and the front there, which (1)
 makes the place before the place 0. Unlike a deque that only grows at its
 back, the places freed at one end serve the insertions at the other, so
 that a deque used as a queue in a steady state never moves its items.

 \section theory_queue_algo Queues with no duplicates and algorithms on a queue

 `UniqueQueue` refuses the duplicates by one of two policies: `IGNORE_NEW`
 does not enqueue an item already in the queue, so that each item keeps the
 place of its first arrival, as the visits of a graph want it, and
 `FORGET_OLD` removes the old copy and enqueues the item at the back, so that
 it keeps the place of its last arrival, as a cache of the least recently
 used items wants it. Either way `enqueue()` must find the copy, which on a
 list costs \f$ O( n ) \f$ comparisons; the invariant is that no item occurs
 twice, which requires an equality that is an equivalence (an item unequal
 to itself, e.g., a NaN, would be enqueued again and again).

 The functions of `QueueAlgorithms.h` act on a queue through its operations
 alone. Moving the positive items of a queue \f$ q \f$ to another one in
 their order dequeues each item of \f$ q \f$ once, \f$ O( n ) \f$; copying
 them instead, with \f$ q \f$ left as it was, enqueues each item in an
 auxiliary queue as it is dequeued and moves the auxiliary queue back,
 which, by the order FIFO of both, restores the order of \f$ q \f$, i.e.,
 \f$ O( n ) \f$ time and space. A queue cannot be restored by rotating it
 alone, i.e., by enqueueing each item again in \f$ q \f$ as it is dequeued,
 without knowing how many items it holds, which the QUEUE of the book does
 not say.

 \section theory_queue_ods The queues of Morin's book

 `RingDeque` is also the `ArrayDeque` of section 2.4 of Morin's Open Data
 Structures, a LIST by rank whose `insert( i , v )` and `erase( i )` move the
 \f$ \min\{ i , n - i \} \f$ items of the shorter side (see
 \ref theory_sequence_ods for the other deques of chapter 2 of Morin and
 the costs of their operations by rank).

 The `RandomQueue` of exercise 2.2 keeps its items in a vector in no order:
 `push()` appends, `pop()` draws a place \f$ j \f$ uniformly, exchanges it
 with the last place and removes the last one, in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$. Each item
 inside is equally likely to come out, whatever the history, since the draw
 does not depend on the order of the vector. The place is drawn by the
 multiplication of Lemire: with \f$ w \f$ a word of 64 bits uniform and
 \f$ w \cdot n = 2^{64} h + l \f$, the high half \f$ h \f$ is uniform in
 \f$ [ 0 , n ) \f$ once the words with \f$ l < ( 2^{64} - n ) \bmod n \f$ are
 rejected, since each value of \f$ h \f$ is then the image of exactly
 \f$ \lfloor 2^{64} / n \rfloor \f$ words; the rejection happens with
 probability below \f$ n / 2^{64} \f$, and the division that computes the
 threshold only when \f$ l < n \f$, i.e., almost never.

 <em>Lemma 1 (two stacks make a queue, exercise 3.15).</em> Let `MinQueue`
 enqueue on a stack \f$ I \f$ and dequeue from a stack \f$ O \f$, moving all
 the items of \f$ I \f$ to \f$ O \f$ when \f$ O \f$ is empty. Then the items
 leave in the order in which they entered, \f$ m \f$ operations take
 \f$ O( m ) \f$ time, and the least item is the lesser of the minima of the
 two stacks, each kept by a `MinStack` (see \ref theory_stack_ods).

 Indeed, the move reverses \f$ I \f$, so that the oldest item is on top of
 \f$ O \f$, and the items of \f$ O \f$ are all older than those of
 \f$ I \f$; each item is pushed and popped twice at most, once on each
 stack, which bounds the total work by \f$ 4 m \f$ operations of the
 stacks. The worst case of one operation is \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$, which the
 real-time queue of Hood and Melville removes by spreading the reversal
 over the operations (see \ref theory_queue_minimum).

 <em>Lemma 2 (two stacks make a deque, exercise 3.16).</em> Let `MinDeque`
 keep a stack \f$ F \f$ with the front on top and a stack \f$ B \f$ with the
 back on top, and let a removal from an empty side deal the \f$ k \f$ items
 of the other side again, the \f$ \lceil k / 2 \rceil \f$ nearer to the end
 of the empty side to it. Then \f$ m \f$ operations take \f$ O( m ) \f$
 time.

 Indeed, let \f$ \Phi = 2 \, | |F| - |B| | \f$. An operation that does not
 deal costs 1 and changes \f$ \Phi \f$ by 2 at most; a deal of \f$ k \f$
 items costs \f$ O( k ) \f$ and is called when one side is empty, i.e.,
 \f$ \Phi = 2 k \f$, after which \f$ \Phi \le 2 \f$ (the removal that
 follows it changes it by 2 more at most), so that the drop of
 \f$ \Phi \f$ pays for it. As for the queue, the least item is the lesser
 of the minima of the two stacks.

 \subsection theory_queue_minimum The minimum in constant worst-case time

 Lemmas 1 and 2 bound sequences, not operations: one `dequeue()` of
 `MinQueue` may move the whole back, one removal of `MinDeque` deal a whole
 side. `RealTimeMinQueue` and `RealTimeMinDeque` spread that work over the
 operations that precede the moment it is needed, on arrays that hold in
 each cell the place of the least item at or below it, allocated with
 their final size when the work starts and emptied one cell at a time, so
 that no operation copies, moves or destroys more than a constant number
 of items (an allocation counted as \f$ O( 1 ) \f$).

 <em>Lemma 3 (the rotation of Hood and Melville).</em> Let
 `RealTimeMinQueue` keep the front \f$ F \f$ and the back \f$ R \f$, and
 start a rotation when \f$ |R| = |F| + 1 \f$: \f$ R \f$ is frozen, a new
 back takes the insertions, and a new front \f$ N \f$ is built from the
 bottom, by three moves per operation, first the frozen back from its top
 and then \f$ F \f$ from its bottom, while the removals take the top of
 \f$ F \f$; when the moves of \f$ F \f$ meet its top, \f$ N \f$ is the
 front. Then the front never empties during a rotation, every operation
 costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ in the worst case, and the back has at most
 \f$ |F| + 1 \f$ items when the next rotation starts.

 Indeed, with \f$ f = |F| \f$ at the start, the operation that starts the
 rotation and the \f$ k \f$ after it make \f$ 3 ( k + 1 ) \f$ moves and at
 most \f$ k \f$ removals, and the \f$ f + 1 \f$ moves of the back are done
 for \f$ k \ge ( f + 1 ) / 3 - 1 \f$, when \f$ F \f$ still has
 \f$ f - k > 0 \f$ items; the moves of \f$ F \f$ go up from its bottom and
 the removals down from its top, so that they meet, after at most
 \f$ \lceil ( 2 f + 1 ) / 3 \rceil \f$ operations in all. \f$ N \f$ holds
 then the remaining items of \f$ F \f$ above those of the frozen back, in
 the order of the queue, \f$ 2 f + 1 - d \f$ of them after \f$ d \f$
 removals, while the new back holds at most the
 \f$ \lceil ( 2 f + 1 ) / 3 \rceil - d \f$ insertions, fewer; hence the
 next rotation starts again with \f$ |R| = |F| + 1 \f$, and the arrays
 allocated at the start, of \f$ f + |R| \f$ and \f$ f + |R| + 2 \f$ cells,
 are large enough. The least item is the least of the minima of the parts:
 \f$ F \f$ (whose least place, if it has moved, is found in \f$ N \f$ at the
 same offset after the frozen back), the frozen back, \f$ N \f$ and the new
 back, each in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ by the place of its top cell.

 <em>Lemma 4 (the global rebuilding of a deque).</em> Let
 `RealTimeMinDeque` keep the stacks \f$ F \f$ and \f$ B \f$ as `MinDeque`
 does, and start a rebuild when one side \f$ G \f$ has \f$ 3 m + 2 \f$
 items, \f$ m \f$ those of the other side \f$ S \f$, or a side fills half
 its array: two arrays of \f$ 2 n_0 + 16 \f$ cells take copies, twelve per
 operation, of the \f$ t = |G| - \lceil n_0 / 2 \rceil \f$ bottom items of
 \f$ G \f$ and of \f$ S \f$ for the new \f$ S \f$, and of the rest of
 \f$ G \f$ for the new \f$ G \f$, as prefixes that a removal shortens by
 one when it goes below their end. Then, for \f$ n_0 \ge 32 \f$, the
 rebuild ends within \f$ n_0 / 11 + 2 \f$ operations, during which no side
 empties and \f$ G \f$ keeps its \f$ t \f$ bottom items, every operation
 costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ in the worst case, and the next rebuild comes
 \f$ \Omega( n_0 ) \f$ operations later.

 Indeed, the copies are at most \f$ n_0 \f$ plus one per insertion during
 the rebuild, so that \f$ k \f$ operations finish them once
 \f$ 12 k \ge n_0 + k \f$, i.e., \f$ k \le n_0 / 11 + 1 \f$ after the
 operation that starts it. At the start of a rebuild by the sizes,
 \f$ m \ge ( n_0 - 4 ) / 4 \ge 7 \f$, more than the removals from
 \f$ S \f$, and \f$ G \f$ keeps \f$ \lceil n_0 / 2 \rceil \f$ items above
 its bottom \f$ t \f$, more than the removals from it; a rebuild by the
 room starts with sides that differ by a factor 3 at most, and the half of
 the arrays that is free holds the insertions. Since the old stacks keep
 all their cells below the tops, their minima stay right, and the
 operations act on them as on `MinDeque` with no deal. When the prefixes
 reach the tops the new arrays replace the old ones, each side with about
 \f$ n_0 / 2 \f$ items, within \f$ n_0 / 11 + 2 \f$ of it, and with room for
 \f$ n_0 + 8 \f$ before half; a new rebuild needs \f$ |G| - 3 |S| \f$ to
 grow from about \f$ - n_0 \f$ to 2, by 3 at most per operation, or a side
 to grow by about \f$ n_0 / 2 \f$, i.e., \f$ \Omega( n_0 ) \f$ operations,
 more than the \f$ ( 2 n_0 + n_0 / 11 + 2 ) / 12 \f$ that destroy the old
 arrays, twelve cells per operation. A deque of fewer than 32 items is
 rebuilt at once, in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$. The items are copied, since the old
 stacks stay in use during the rebuild, while the queue moves them.

 \subsection theory_queue_sliding The aggregate of a sliding window

 `SlidingAggregate< G , Policy >` is a queue of values \f$ x_1 , \ldots ,
 x_n \f$ of a monoid \f$ ( G , \oplus , e ) \f$, from the front to the back,
 with `aggregate()`, the combination \f$ \alpha = x_1 \oplus \cdots \oplus
 x_n \f$ in this order (\f$ e \f$ if \f$ n = 0 \f$), which the queues with the
 minimum above compute for \f$ \oplus = \min \f$. The algebra and the
 policy choose one of four algorithms (see \ref theory_ADS_algebra), whose
 correctness follows.

 <em>Lemma 5 (subtract-on-evict).</em> In a group, the aggregate kept as
 \f$ s \gets s \oplus x \f$ at each insertion of \f$ x \f$ at the back and
 \f$ s \gets x_1^{-1} \oplus s \f$ at each removal of \f$ x_1 \f$ from the
 front equals \f$ \alpha \f$, each operation in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ combinations.

 Indeed, by induction on the operations, from \f$ s = e \f$: an insertion
 makes \f$ s = x_1 \oplus \cdots \oplus x_n \oplus x \f$, and a removal
 makes \f$ x_1^{-1} \oplus x_1 \oplus ( x_2 \oplus \cdots \oplus x_n ) \f$,
 i.e., Lemma 3 (iii) of \ref theory_ADS_algebra; no commutativity is used,
 and with floating point values the aggregate is recomputed from the items
 every `period()` removals, at \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized cost when the period
 is at least \f$ n \f$.

 <em>Lemma 6 (the monotone deque).</em> In a selective monoid, let the
 deque \f$ C \f$ of the candidates be updated at each insertion of \f$ x \f$
 by removing from its back each \f$ c \f$ with \f$ c \oplus x = x \f$ and
 then appending \f$ x \f$, and at each removal of \f$ x_1 \f$ by removing its
 front if it is \f$ x_1 \f$ (as an item, by its number). Then, for every
 \f$ l \f$, the combination of the candidates of numbers at least \f$ l \f$,
 in their order, is \f$ x_l \oplus \cdots \oplus x_n \f$, and the front of
 \f$ C \f$ is \f$ \alpha \f$; each operation costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized.

 Indeed, by induction on the operations. Let \f$ \Gamma_l \f$ be the
 combination of the candidates of numbers at least \f$ l \f$. At an
 insertion of \f$ x \f$, a removed candidate \f$ c \f$ is the last one, so
 that \f$ \Gamma_l = \Gamma' \oplus c \f$ with \f$ \Gamma' \f$ the
 candidates before it, and \f$ x_l \oplus \cdots \oplus x_n \oplus x =
 \Gamma' \oplus ( c \oplus x ) = \Gamma' \oplus x \f$; once no candidate is
 removed, appending \f$ x \f$ gives \f$ \Gamma_l \oplus x \f$, which is the
 new suffix. At a removal the suffixes from \f$ l \ge 2 \f$ are those of
 before, and the candidate of number 1, if there is one, goes. Two
 consecutive candidates \f$ c , c' \f$ became consecutive when \f$ c' \f$
 was appended with \f$ c \f$ last, and then \f$ c \oplus c' \ne c' \f$,
 i.e., \f$ c \oplus c' = c \f$ by selectivity; hence, combining from the
 back, \f$ \Gamma_1 \f$ is the front of \f$ C \f$. Each item enters and
 leaves \f$ C \f$ once.

 <em>Lemma 7 (Two-Stacks Lite).</em> In any monoid, let the items be in one
 deque whose first \f$ m \f$ items carry the aggregates
 \f$ S_k = x_k \oplus \cdots \oplus x_m \f$, \f$ k \le m \f$, and let
 \f$ b \f$ be the aggregate of the others; an insertion combines its item
 into \f$ b \f$, and a removal with \f$ m = 0 \f$ first makes all the items
 the first part, computing \f$ S_n , S_{n-1} , \ldots , S_1 \f$ from the
 back and setting \f$ b = e \f$. Then \f$ \alpha = S_1 \oplus b \f$, and each
 operation costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized (Tangwongsan, Hirzel and
 Schneider).

 Indeed, \f$ S_1 \f$ covers the items of the first part and \f$ b \f$ those
 after it, in the order of the queue; a removal drops \f$ x_1 \f$ and
 \f$ S_1 \f$, and the next \f$ S_2 \f$ is still the aggregate of the first
 part, while an insertion extends \f$ b \f$ on the right. Each item enters
 the first part once, at the cost of one combination, which the potential
 \f$ n - m \f$ pays.

 <em>Lemma 8 (the real-time window).</em> Let the queue be kept as
 `RealTimeMinQueue` keeps it (Lemma 3), each cell of a front (\f$ F \f$,
 \f$ N \f$) holding the aggregate of its item and of the cells below it
 with the item first, \f$ x \oplus \sigma \f$, and each cell of a back
 (\f$ R \f$, the frozen \f$ R' \f$) the aggregate with the item last,
 \f$ \sigma \oplus x \f$. Then, with \f$ \hat P \f$ the aggregate in the top
 cell of the part \f$ P \f$ and \f$ \hat N_k \f$ that of the cell
 \f$ k - 1 \f$ of \f$ N \f$, \f$ k = \min( r_0 , |N| ) \f$,
 \f[
   \alpha = \hat F \oplus \hat R' \oplus \hat N_k \oplus \hat R ,
 \f]
 the parts that are empty left out, and every operation costs
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ in the worst case.

 Indeed, the cost is that of Lemma 3, plus one combination per move. The
 top cell of \f$ F \f$ holds the aggregate of the cells from 0 to its top,
 in the order of the queue, including the bottom cells already moved into
 \f$ N \f$, whose items are still in the queue right after those of the
 cells that remain (the aggregate was computed when the cells were pushed,
 and the moves do not change it), and excluding the items removed from the
 top. The top of \f$ R' \f$ covers the items of the frozen back that are not
 yet moved, which precede in the queue those moved; the first \f$ k \f$
 cells of \f$ N \f$ hold these, the last item of the back at the bottom, so
 that the aggregate of the cell \f$ k - 1 \f$, the item first, combines them
 in the order of the queue; the cells of \f$ N \f$ above \f$ r_0 \f$ hold
 items of \f$ F \f$ already counted in \f$ \hat F \f$. The new back follows.
 When the rotation ends \f$ N \f$ is the front, its top cell covering all
 its items.

 The minimum of `RealTimeMinQueue` is Lemma 8 for the selective monoid of
 the minima, where the parts may overlap since \f$ \min \f$ is idempotent,
 which is why it reads the least place of \f$ F \f$ in \f$ N \f$ instead.
 `MinQueue` and `RealTimeMinQueue` stay, since they also take an order
 that is an object and give the least item by reference, which a monoid
 whose combination returns a value cannot; the test `sliding-minqueue`
 checks that the windows of the minima agree with them.

 \section theory_queue_refs References

